{"author":"Unknown","author_email":"","data":{"advisory":[{"agreement":1,"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Confirm/document that test_live_distinct_sources_report_no_clone relies on jscpd's default exact (non-normalized) tokenization so structurally-identical bodies with distinct identifiers report 0 clones.","criteria":["edge-cases","maintainability"],"decision":"advisory","evidence":["tests/unit/metrics/test_jscpd_dup_heldout.py:129: `f\"  const {prefix}_{index} = ({prefix}_seed + {index}) * {index + 2};\\n\"`","tests/unit/metrics/test_jscpd_dup_heldout.py:169: `assert result.duplication[\"clones\"] == 0`","The adapter runs jscpd with only default flags (src/rebar/metrics/analyzers/_jscpd.py:28-35), so the negative control depends on jscpd's default matching mode treating differing identifiers as distinct tokens."],"finding":"The negative-control test test_live_distinct_sources_report_no_clone asserts clones == 0 for two files that are structurally identical (same statement scaffolding: `const … = (…seed + i) * (i+2);`) differing only by identifier prefix. This is correct only under jscpd's default exact/type-1 tokenization where identifiers are distinct tokens; if the detector applied identifier normalization the structurally-identical bodies could still be flagged, making this assertion fail. The reliance is undocumented in the test.","id":"0","impact":0.3,"location":"tests/unit/metrics/test_jscpd_dup_heldout.py:163","norm_id":"n8ac6cc3840a4c338","priority":0.225,"reason":"default-advisory","scenarios":["jscpd configured/upgraded to normalize identifiers","future adapter change adding a normalization flag"],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.75,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_nece